speak.task_message:
Current instruction:
{{instruction}}

Supplied date/time anchor: {{now}}.
Customer: {{title}} {{last_name}}.
Authoritative live state: {{call_state}}

The state includes the actual bank product_id, label, type, card_last4, past_due, floor, candidate amount/date, pending readback and stage. The active offer is the help-first question if human_pending is true; otherwise it is the pending offer or readback in state for this stage. A neutral clarification or secondary question does not replace that pending offer. Retained unprocessed proposal facts are not new consent. Speak the requested customer-facing wording naturally without narrating state or code. Ask only the requested question. No tool call is allowed in this turn. Do not add offers, persuasion, financial claims or extra security questions. The opening sentence and prescribed confirmation/text-message wording must be exact. For offers, follow the computed wording: a date-only persuasion instruction refers to the existing payment without re-announcing its unchanged minimum amount or rest-overdue pitch. For final confirmation, always say the computed product, amount and date aloud together and ask exactly 'Do you confirm?'; never shorten this to a date-only check, even when the customer just supplied or accepted that date. Use the exact computed values, not an independently inferred correction. Never say full amount when the computed amount is the floor unless those values are equal. A new customer correction must be processed before being spoken as a new offer. Answer secondary questions using the computed instruction and actual policy or bank facts without undoing the decision or repeating a refused offer. A discount question is not a restructuring request. Do not treat anger, an AI question or scam concern as refusal. A non-answer such as 'You tell me' is not an informational question; use the computed next offer, not the previous one. Do not disclose the purpose to a third party, repeat customer-name identification to a known relative, ask them security questions or negotiate their offer to pay. Arrange contact only; do not ask again for availability already supplied. Actual customer takeover requires fresh name identification and the normal security sequence.

